% Findings from the Rust-lane audit of the remediation (2026-09-30): each was a
% disagreement between hosts that no earlier fixture pinned. Value-level cases; the
% SQL ones are in sql/cases/52-audit-cases.sqlt and the dependencies() ones are API
% probes (tools/api.* and tools/api-pins.txt).

### name: text.replace.cap-is-counted-in-code-points
--- note
The cap is on code points (SPEC 6.4). REPLACE replaces every U+1F600 (four bytes,
one code point) with "ab" (two bytes, two code points), so the text GROWS: 8388609
of them make 16777218 code points. Go compared byte lengths, saw "ab" as the
shorter one and skipped the check.
--- source
LEN(REPLACE("😀", "ab", REPEAT("😀", 8388609)))
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:5
===

### name: text.replace.cap-control-exactly-at-the-cap
--- note
One fewer and the result is exactly 16777216 code points, which is allowed.
--- source
LEN(REPLACE("😀", "ab", REPEAT("😀", 8388608)))
--- expect
num 16777216
===

### name: text.rreplace.cap-is-counted-in-code-points
--- note
The same growth through RREPLACE, with a two-code-point replacement for a one-code-point match.
--- source
LEN(RREPLACE("😀", "ab", REPEAT("😀", 8388609)))
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:5
===

### name: link.predicate-appending-to-its-source-visits-a-snapshot
--- note
A join walks each side as it stood when the join started (SPEC 7.3). The predicate grows X on every pair, but there are still 2 x 2 pairs. JS raised a TypeError, Python 'dictionary changed size during iteration', PHP and C++ counted 6 and Lisp ran until the collection cap.
--- source
X = LIST(1, 2); COUNT(LINK(X, X, A, B, (X[COUNT(X)+1]=1; TRUE)))
--- expect
num 4
===

### name: link.predicate-appending-to-the-right-side-visits-a-snapshot
--- note
The right side is walked again for every left row, so a live walk gave the second left row more right rows.
--- source
X = LIST(1, 2); Y = LIST(3, 4); COUNT(LINK(X, Y, A, B, (Y[COUNT(Y)+1]=1; TRUE)))
--- expect
num 4
===

### name: link.predicate-appending-to-the-left-side-visits-a-snapshot
--- note
The left side is listed once as well.
--- source
X = LIST(1, 2); Y = LIST(3, 4); COUNT(LINK(X, Y, A, B, (X[COUNT(X)+1]=1; TRUE)))
--- expect
num 4
===

### name: link-left.predicate-appending-to-its-source-visits-a-snapshot
--- note
LINK_LEFT walks the same snapshots.
--- source
X = LIST(1, 2); COUNT(LINK_LEFT(X, X, A, B, (X[COUNT(X)+1]=1; TRUE)))
--- expect
num 4
===

### name: link.equi-join-residual-appending-to-its-source-visits-a-snapshot
--- note
The equality is a hash key and the rest a residual predicate; the residual grows X, and the answer is still the two matching pairs.
--- source
X = LIST(1, 2); COUNT(LINK(X, X, A, B, A == B AND (X[COUNT(X)+1]=1; TRUE)))
--- expect
num 2
===

### name: re.icase.nonascii-pattern-is-a-run-time-refusal
--- note
A non-ASCII pattern under `i` is E_BAD_ARG at run time whatever else it holds
(SPEC 7.8), so the compile-time analysis applies the `i` fold only to an ASCII
pattern. `(?:k|K)+` is exponentially ambiguous once `i` folds k and K together, and
JS, C++ and Python refused the pattern at compile time for that; the others gave the
run-time error the spec names, at the flag.
--- source
RMATCH('(?:k|K)+é', "x", "i")
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG at 1:26
===

### name: re.icase.nonascii-pattern-in-a-dead-branch-compiles
--- note
The same pattern in a branch that never runs is not an error: only the run-time refusal exists for it.
--- source
IF(FALSE, RMATCH('(?:k|K)+é', "x", "i"), 1)
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: re.posix.unterminated-form-is-refused.1
--- note
A `[` followed by `:`, `.` or `=` inside a class is refused whether or not the closer follows (SPEC 7.8): the engines read even an unfinished POSIX form differently.
--- source
RMATCH('[x[:y]', "x")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX at 1:8
===

### name: re.posix.unterminated-form-is-refused.2
--- note
A `[` followed by `:`, `.` or `=` inside a class is refused whether or not the closer follows (SPEC 7.8): the engines read even an unfinished POSIX form differently.
--- source
RMATCH('[[:a]', "x")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX at 1:8
===

### name: re.posix.unterminated-form-is-refused.3
--- note
A `[` followed by `:`, `.` or `=` inside a class is refused whether or not the closer follows (SPEC 7.8): the engines read even an unfinished POSIX form differently.
--- source
RMATCH('[[.]', "x")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX at 1:8
===

### name: re.posix.unterminated-form-is-refused.4
--- note
A `[` followed by `:`, `.` or `=` inside a class is refused whether or not the closer follows (SPEC 7.8): the engines read even an unfinished POSIX form differently.
--- source
RMATCH('[a[=]', "x")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX at 1:8
===

### name: re.posix.escaped-bracket-then-dot-is-a-literal
--- note
The control: an ESCAPED bracket is a literal, so `[a\[.]` is a class of `a`, `[` and `.`.
--- source
RMATCH('[a\[.]', "x")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===

### name: re.range.reversed-in-a-dead-branch-is-refused
--- note
A class range that runs backwards is a compile-time refusal of a literal pattern in a branch that never runs, in every host (C++ answered 1).
--- source
IF(FALSE, RFIND('[z-a]', "x"), 1)
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX at 1:17
===

### name: re.range.reversed-in-a-dead-branch-under-the-i-flag
--- note
The flag changes nothing about a reversed range.
--- source
IF(FALSE, RFIND('[z-a]', "x", "i"), 1)
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX at 1:17
===

### name: join.order.both-sources-raise-left-first
--- note
Sources run as written: the left source of the outer LINK (itself a LINK whose left is NOPE_A) is evaluated before the right source NOPE_C (SPEC 7.4, 6.2). A host that hands the FILTER down through the join and evaluates the right source first reported NOPE_C. 'Pure' means no side effects; errors are observable.
--- source
Y = LIST(RECORD("a",1,"b",2,"q",1)); NOPE_A .> LINK(Y, _1['a'] == _2['a']) .> LINK(NOPE_C, _['b'] == _2['b']) .> FILTER(_['q'] == 1) .> MAP(1)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:38
===

### name: join.order.both-sources-raise-left-first-in-the-helper-form
--- note
The same program with the join assigned first: nothing can be handed down, and the left source raises first.
--- source
Y = LIST(RECORD("a",1,"b",2,"q",1)); J = NOPE_A .> LINK(Y, _1['a'] == _2['a']) .> LINK(NOPE_C, _['b'] == _2['b']); J .> FILTER(_['q'] == 1) .> MAP(1)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:42
===

### name: join.order.only-the-right-source-raises
--- note
The left side is fine, so the right source's own error is the one reported, at its own position.
--- source
Y = LIST(RECORD("a",1,"b",2,"q",1)); Y .> LINK(Y, _1['a'] == _2['a']) .> LINK(NOPE_C, _['b'] == _2['b']) .> FILTER(_['q'] == 1) .> MAP(1)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:79
===

### name: join.order.link-left-both-sources-raise-left-first
--- note
The same through LINK_LEFT as the outer join.
--- source
Y = LIST(RECORD("a",1,"b",2,"q",1)); NOPE_A .> LINK(Y, _1['a'] == _2['a']) .> LINK_LEFT(NOPE_C, _['b'] == _2['b']) .> FILTER(_['q'] == 1) .> MAP(1)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:38
===

### name: join.order.filter-below-the-join-both-raise
--- note
A FILTER (not a LINK) as the left source of the outer join: the left source still runs first.
--- source
Y = LIST(RECORD("a",1,"b",2,"q",1)); NOPE_A .> FILTER(_['q'] == 1) .> LINK(NOPE_C, _['b'] == _2['b']) .> FILTER(_['q'] == 1) .> MAP(1)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:38
===
